  "design": "Built from the open geography (tables/_geo.py, terrain_raster() at pixel resolution) and the open tile sheet's own terrain masks (art/map-terrain.png, 16 x 16 terrain pixels per tile, the same grain as the raster's 16 x 16 pixels per cell). The masks are read from the directory in TILE_ART, or else from the art/ of the pack opendata.py writes. Tiles used. Only bare geography: open sea, plain land, and the coast tiles. Left out: the tiles with a building (68-79, 123-127, 255: towns belong to the town step, which overlays them per era) and the spare sea tiles with white surf (56-59: their mask is open sea, so they would show breakers where a ship sails freely). Tiles 40-55 are open shallows (solid, edges and corners toward the sea) for the reefs and banks (tables/_geo.py, REEFS), the map's only shallows: the shore halo the coast tiles paint is sea in their terrain (tables/tile_terrain.py), so every beach can be sailed up to. Every reef must show on the map, some of its pixels drawn with shallows tiles, or the build stops. Matching. Each cell's 16 x 16 block of the raster is compared pixel by pixel with each tile's mask; a pixel that is land on one side and sea on the other costs LAND_SEA, land against shallows LAND_SHALLOWS, shallows against sea SEA_SHALLOWS. A cell of open sea only takes a plain sea tile, a cell with no water a land tile; a cell with shallows but no land is matched like a coast, among the tiles with no land (no stray land at sea). A cell a navigable channel crosses (tables/_geo.py, CHANNELS) takes no tile with shallows, so the channel stays open to every ship, and nor does a cell with no shallows in the raster: the shallows are the reefs alone, never a stand-in for a sliver of land. Seams. A second pass (iterated conditional modes, cells in two interleaved halves, until nothing changes) lets each coastal cell take, among the tiles within TOLERANCE of its best cost, the one that also best matches its four neighbours' facing edges: each pair of touching edge pixels costs as above, times SEAM. So a coastline drawn on one tile goes on across the border on the next. Every island of the raster keeps land: when no chosen tile over an island's bounding box has land, the cell holding a pixel of it is held to tiles with land and the seams run again. Variety. Among equally good tiles (the 40 open sea and 51 plain land variants above all) a hash of the cell and the tile breaks the tie: varied, and the same on every run. The six eras share this base map (base_grid()); each era's map puts that era's towns on it, the building tiles tables/towns.py chooses. 8237 coastal cells, 647 islands restored, 16 seam sweeps, 0.048% of the pixels shallows; pixels differing from the raster 0.396%: sea as shallows 0.010%, sea as land 0.144%, shallows as sea 0.028%, shallows as land 0.000%, land as sea 0.214%, land as shallows 0.000%."
